EXT. DRY WASH - HIGH DESERT - DAWN

A dry riverbed. Sage, cracked clay, a lone cottonwood bleached white. A horse stands hobbled by the tree, nosing at the dust. A campfire has burned to a gray nest.

A RIDER, fifties, hat low, sits on a flat stone with a tin cup. A graying beard. A slow way of moving, as if he'd spent a lifetime bargaining with his joints.

He tilts the cup. Dregs. He tosses them on the ashes.

RIDER
(to the horse)
Don't look at me like that. It's the last of the coffee.

The horse flicks an ear.

He reaches into his saddlebag and withdraws a folded sheet of paper, brown at the creases. Smooths it on his knee. Reads in silence. Then, aloud:

RIDER (CONT'D)
"Dear Abel. The farm is sold. Your sister has the boy. Do not come back, for there is nothing to come back to."

He lowers the letter. The wind lifts the edge of it. He holds it down with a thumb.

RIDER (CONT'D)
(to the horse)
Abel Corrigan. That's me. You never asked.

The horse snorts.

ABEL
Well. Now you know.

He folds the letter along its creases and tucks it into his hatband. He gets to his feet in stages.

ABEL (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Forty miles to the river.

From somewhere a long way off, down the wash, comes the thin whistle of a train.

ABEL (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Would you listen to that.

He takes the horse's reins. He leads it a few paces, stops, and stands listening until the whistle fades.

ABEL (V.O.)
(his own, young, reading)
"Dear Father. We are well. The boy has your eyes."

ABEL
(softly)
Hush now.

He mounts, slowly, and rides out along the wash toward the east, where the sun has begun to lift itself over the ridge.
